<p>Most writing about hair transplant recovery describes the year, which the <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-growth-timeline-month-by-month">month-by-month growth timeline</a> covers. This article covers only the fortnight, because the fortnight is when the wound is real, the grafts are physically vulnerable, and you have to decide whether you can be seen in an office. Protocols differ between clinics, so read this alongside the <a href="/hair-transplant-aftercare">clinic aftercare guidance</a> and follow your own written instructions where the two disagree.</p>

<h2 id="days-0-to-2-swelling-and-the-first-night">Days 0 to 2: swelling, and why the first night matters</h2>

<p>You leave with the donor area dressed and the recipient area open. Small dots of dried blood between the grafts are expected on the evening of day 0, and light oozing overnight is normal. You will be given a saline spray and told to use it often. That is not a comfort measure; it stops the grafts drying out.</p>

<p>Sleep propped up at roughly forty-five degrees, on your back, for the first two to three nights. A recliner works, or pillows with a travel pillow round the neck. Two things are being managed. The first is contact: newly placed grafts sit in shallow incisions, held by a fibrin clot rather than by anything mechanical, so a face-down pillow can shift them. The second is fluid, which tracks downwards under gravity — hence forehead swelling that usually appears on day two, peaks around day three or four, and can reach the eyelids before draining away. It looks alarming and is almost always harmless. A cold compress helps, on the forehead and brows only, never over the grafted area.</p>

<h2 id="the-first-wash">The first wash, and how much pressure is safe</h2>

<p>Most clinics schedule the first wash between day two and day four, and many perform it for you so that you see the technique rather than read it. The principle: water should fall onto the scalp, not be aimed at it.</p>

<ul>
  <li>An emollient left on the recipient area for twenty to thirty minutes, softening the crusts before any water touches them.</li>
  <li>Lukewarm water poured from a mug or a cupped hand. No shower jet on the recipient area, on any setting.</li>
  <li>A mild shampoo lathered in your palm, then laid onto the grafts and rinsed away. No fingertip pressure, no nails, no rubbing in the first week.</li>
  <li>The donor area may be washed more normally, because it is a closed wound rather than a bed of loose grafts.</li>
  <li>Patting dry with a clean towel, or air drying.</li>
</ul>

<p>From roughly day ten the wash changes character: gentle circular fingertip massage is usually permitted, and that is what lifts the remaining crusts. The date you may start rubbing is the most important instruction of the fortnight, and it belongs to your surgeon rather than to a video.</p>

<h2 id="days-3-to-7-scabbing-redness-and-itching">Days 3 to 7: scabbing, redness and itching</h2>

<p>By day three the recipient area is covered in small crusts, one over each graft, with pink skin between them, and the donor is scabbing in a different pattern. Around day four or five the itching starts, which is healing rather than anything wrong.</p>

<p>This is the window in which grafts are genuinely lost, and they go to ordinary domestic accidents rather than to anything medical:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Pulling a t-shirt or kurta over the head. Wear shirts that button, for a fortnight.</li>
  <li>Rolling onto the recipient area in your sleep, or scratching in it. Night-time itch relief is worth asking about rather than enduring.</li>
  <li>Towel drying out of habit.</li>
  <li>Heavy lifting, straining and bending at the waist, which raise scalp pressure while swelling is still settling.</li>
  <li>A car headrest, a helmet, a mask strap dragged across the temples, or a barber's chair.</li>
</ul>

<p>A graft dislodged in this window does not regrow, and the follicle it came from cannot be replaced. Donor supply is finite, as <a href="/blog/donor-area-limits-hair-transplant">the one-time donor budget</a> sets out, so a graft lost to a towel is spent for good.</p>

<h2 id="days-7-to-14-crust-clearance">Days 7 to 14: crusts clearing, and hairs that come away with them</h2>

<p>Somewhere between day eight and day fourteen the crusts lift, usually with the gentle massage your clinic has by then allowed. Each tends to come away with a short dark hair attached, and this is the moment almost every patient sends a photograph in a panic.</p>

<p>What has come away is the hair shaft. The follicle sits several millimetres deeper in the skin, anchored in the incision, and is not attached to the crust in any meaningful way. Losing the shaft is expected, and it begins the shed that continues into weeks three and four; native hairs thinning around the grafts is a separate matter, dealt with in <a href="/blog/shedding-and-shock-loss-after-hair-transplant">what shedding and shock loss actually mean</a>.</p>

<p>By day fourteen most people have a largely clear recipient area, some pinkness, and no visible hair where the grafts were. That is expected, not a setback: judging a result now is like judging one from a photograph taken on the operating table, a trap <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">reading before-and-after photographs honestly</a> describes.</p>

<h2 id="picking-scratching-and-hats">Picking, scratching and hats</h2>

<p>Picking is the commonest self-inflicted problem of the fortnight. A crust removed before it is ready takes skin with it, may take the graft, and drives bacteria from under a fingernail into an open follicle. If a crust is stubborn on day twelve, soften it and wash again the next day rather than working at it.</p>

<h3>Hats</h3>

<p>A loose hat is usually permitted from around day ten to fourteen, but the risk is not the hat sitting on your head. It is the two seconds of putting it on and taking it off, when the brim drags across the grafts. Lower it straight down, lift it straight up, and avoid elastic bands. Tight caps and motorcycle helmets are a separate conversation, and in this city an unavoidable one, which is why <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-recovery-in-gurgaon">recovering through Gurgaon heat, dust and helmets</a> is worth reading before you pick a date.</p>

<h2 id="how-the-donor-area-behaves">How the donor area behaves separately</h2>

<p>The donor area follows its own schedule and usually finishes first. After <a href="/fue-hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">follicular unit extraction</a>, the back and sides carry hundreds of tiny circular wounds that scab within days and are commonly closed by day seven to ten. It often feels tight rather than sore, and numbness or tingling is common; that can take weeks or months to settle, and sits among the ordinary <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-side-effects-and-risks">expected effects rather than the complications</a>.</p>

<p>If the donor was shaved, stubble growing back through it hides the area better than anything you can apply. It is also the part you cannot see, so have someone photograph it every few days rather than guessing in a mirror.</p>

