<h2 id="going-back-to-work-and-being-seen">Desk work, video calls and when people stop noticing</h2>

<p>Nobody can promise you are invisible by a particular date, though the shape of the fortnight is predictable. Days one and two are the least conspicuous; there is redness, but it reads as irritation. Days two to four are the swelling peak, and this is the stage colleagues comment on, because it changes the face rather than the scalp. Days five to ten are the crusting peak, unmistakably post-operative at conversational distance. From about day twelve, once crusts have cleared, most people with a shaved head look recently shaved rather than recently operated on.</p>

<p>Small adjustments help. Work from home across the swelling and crusting peak if you can. Tilt the laptop camera level with your face rather than down onto your scalp, and sit with a light in front of you rather than overhead, which picks out crusting. If your work is physical, dusty or involves a helmet, plan the leave properly and look at <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">how a hair transplant is planned and scheduled</a> before committing to a date. Placement method changes little here, though <a href="/blog/fue-vs-dhi-hair-transplant">the practical differences between FUE and DHI</a> come up often at this point.</p>

<h2 id="what-warrants-a-call">What warrants a call rather than waiting for your review</h2>

<p>Most of the fortnight is unremarkable, and most messages a clinic receives are about entirely normal things. A small number are not. Contact the clinic, rather than waiting for the review or asking a forum, if any of these appear:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Fever, or a general sense of being unwell.</li>
  <li>Pain that increases after day three instead of settling, particularly if confined to one area.</li>
  <li>Spreading redness with warmth, a hot swollen area that is not symmetrical, pus, or an offensive smell.</li>
  <li>Bleeding that does not stop after ten minutes of gentle, steady pressure with clean gauze.</li>
  <li>A graft that has clearly come out, especially with fresh bleeding from the site.</li>
  <li>A rash, breathlessness or facial swelling after starting a prescribed medicine.</li>
  <li>Donor wound edges separating, or any area that looks as though it is not closing.</li>
</ul>

<p>None is common, all are easier to manage early, and the call costs nothing. Numbers and hours are on the <a href="/contact">clinic contact page</a>, and how you reach a doctor after hours is worth asking before you book.</p>

<h2 id="what-the-fortnight-does-not-tell-you">What this fortnight does not tell you</h2>

<p>Healing well is not the same as growing well. A textbook fourteen days tells you the wound has closed. It says nothing about density, because nothing has grown yet. Nor does a good recovery pause the underlying process: androgenetic alopecia continues in untreated native hair whether or not you have had surgery (<a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/male-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet: male pattern hair loss</a>), which is why medical treatment is usually discussed alongside surgery rather than instead of it.</p>

<p>The judgement belongs to month ten or twelve, the interval any honest gallery states, including the <a href="/hair-transplant-results-gurgaon">documented patient results at ten to twelve months</a>. Your job for the next fortnight is narrower than it feels: keep the area clean, keep your hands off it, sleep propped up, and turn up to the review.</p>

